WEBVTT

00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:15,900
any particular reason why my oncologist is ordering a PET scan after my four-week chemo holiday

00:00:16,540 --> 00:00:25,020
versus a CT scan, which I normally use to. Um, yes, that because that is standard procedure.

00:00:25,020 --> 00:00:30,460
So I can't remember if you received a PET scan before or not, but that's irrelevant.

00:00:30,460 --> 00:00:35,900
Typical standard procedure is your, once you're diagnosed with cancer, they're going to start

00:00:35,900 --> 00:00:42,140
chemotherapy, then typically a month after chemotherapy, you'll receive a PET scan.

00:00:42,780 --> 00:00:50,300
And typically insurance won't cover a PET scan until after chemotherapy.

00:00:50,860 --> 00:00:55,660
And the oncologist wants to know if there is metastasis anywhere else.

00:00:57,260 --> 00:01:01,660
And if you didn't receive a PET scan prior to chemotherapy,

00:01:01,660 --> 00:01:09,260
then he can compare if there was metastasis prior to chemotherapy versus post chemotherapy.

00:01:09,260 --> 00:01:14,140
So that's the purpose of ordering a PET scan. Again, the value of a PET scan

00:01:14,140 --> 00:01:19,500
is that you're going to see if there's an increased level of metabolic uptake

00:01:20,860 --> 00:01:27,420
of the glucose from the PET scan in all throughout the entire body versus a CT scan,

00:01:27,980 --> 00:01:37,660
which is just looking at structure, um, tissue structure in the area of the CT scan in its region.

00:01:37,660 --> 00:01:43,340
So a CT scan is regional. Maybe we're going to do it abdominal or upper abdominal CT scan.

00:01:44,460 --> 00:01:48,060
And but a PET scan is, um, throughout your whole body.

